/* Bondline Daily: one chemistry article a day. The article for a date is ARTICLES[(days since START) mod length].
   Fields: s slug, c category, t title, h one-line hook, p paragraphs (simple HTML), x exam link, k topic keywords. */
window.BL_DAILY_START='2026-11-07';
window.BL_DAILY=[
{s:'onions-make-you-cry',c:'Food',t:'Why chopping onions makes you cry',
 h:'An onion is a chemical weapon that only arms itself when you cut it.',
 p:['An intact onion barely smells. Its cells keep a sulfur-containing amino acid derivative, isoalliin, in one compartment and an enzyme, alliinase, in another. Your knife breaks the walls between them.',
 'Alliinase splits isoalliin into 1-propenesulfenic acid. A second enzyme, lachrymatory-factor synthase, quickly rearranges that into <b>syn-propanethial-S-oxide</b>, a small, volatile molecule that drifts up to your eyes.',
 'There it reacts with the water on the surface of your eye and stimulates the nerve endings that sense irritation. Your tear glands respond by flushing it away.',
 'Every trick that helps works on that chemistry: chilling the onion slows the enzymes and makes the gas less volatile, a sharp knife breaks fewer cells, and cutting under a fan or near running water carries the gas away.'],
 x:'Enzymes as catalysts; volatility and intermolecular forces.',k:'enzymes, sulfur compounds, volatility'},
{s:'smell-of-rain',c:'Smell',t:'The smell of rain is a bacterium',
 h:'That earthy smell after rain is a molecule your nose can detect at a few parts per trillion.',
 p:['The smell has a name, petrichor, and its main ingredient is <b>geosmin</b>, a bicyclic alcohol made by soil bacteria called Streptomyces and by some algae.',
 'When raindrops hit dry soil they trap tiny pockets of air that burst upwards as aerosols, carrying geosmin and other compounds into the air you breathe. Light rain on dry ground makes the most aerosols, which is why the first shower after a dry spell smells strongest.',
 'Humans are extraordinarily sensitive to geosmin: in water, we can detect it at just a few parts per trillion. It is also why beetroot tastes earthy and why water from a reservoir sometimes tastes "muddy", even though it is harmless.',
 'Geosmin is an alcohol with a rigid two-ring skeleton, and that compact, greasy shape suits the odour receptor that binds it.'],
 x:'Functional groups (alcohols); concentration in parts per million and billion.',k:'odour, alcohols, concentration'},
{s:'most-bitter-substance',c:'Record',t:'The most bitter substance ever made',
 h:'Nintendo coats Switch game cards with it so that children spit them out.',
 p:['<b>Denatonium benzoate</b> is the most bitter compound known. Most people can taste it at around ten parts per billion, and at a few parts per million it is unbearable.',
 'It was discovered by accident in 1958 by chemists working on local anaesthetics related to lidocaine. Structurally it is a quaternary ammonium salt: a nitrogen with four carbon groups and a permanent positive charge, paired with a benzoate anion.',
 'That bitterness is useful. It is added to antifreeze, cleaning products, nail-biting deterrents and denatured alcohol so that people and pets do not swallow them. Nintendo Switch game cards are coated with it for the same reason.',
 'Bitterness is detected by a family of about 25 receptor proteins on your tongue. Denatonium activates several of them strongly, which is part of why it is so intense.'],
 x:'Ionic compounds and quaternary ammonium salts; amines.',k:'amines, salts, taste'},
{s:'helium-found-in-the-sun',c:'History',t:'Helium was found in the Sun before it was found on Earth',
 h:'For 27 years, helium was an element that only existed in sunlight.',
 p:['During a solar eclipse in 1868, astronomers looking at the light from the Sun’s edge through a spectroscope saw a bright yellow line at a wavelength of about 588 nm. It was close to the famous sodium lines but did not match any known element.',
 'Norman Lockyer proposed that it came from a new element and named it after the Greek word for the Sun, <i>helios</i>.',
 'It was not until 1895 that William Ramsay found helium on Earth, released from a uranium mineral called cleveite. Helium on Earth comes mostly from alpha particles emitted by radioactive decay, trapped underground in natural gas.',
 'The story shows the power of emission spectra: every element emits light at its own set of wavelengths, so light from a star tells you what it is made of.'],
 x:'Atomic emission spectra and energy levels.',k:'spectroscopy, electrons, energy levels'},
{s:'thalidomide',c:'Medicine',t:'Thalidomide and the mirror-image drug',
 h:'Two molecules with the same atoms, the same bonds and very different effects.',
 p:['Thalidomide was sold from 1957 as a sedative and for morning sickness. More than ten thousand babies were born with severe limb defects before it was withdrawn in 1961.',
 'Thalidomide has a chiral centre, so it exists as two enantiomers, mirror images that cannot be superimposed. It was sold as a 50:50 mixture (a racemate). Research later linked the (S) enantiomer most strongly to the birth defects.',
 'A common myth is that selling only the "safe" (R) enantiomer would have prevented the disaster. It would not: the hydrogen on the chiral centre is acidic, so in the body the two forms interconvert within hours.',
 'The tragedy changed drug regulation worldwide, and it is why regulators now ask companies to study each enantiomer of a chiral drug separately.'],
 x:'Optical isomerism, enantiomers and racemic mixtures.',k:'chirality, enantiomers, drugs'},
{s:'spearmint-and-caraway',c:'Smell',t:'Spearmint and caraway are mirror images',
 h:'Your nose can tell left from right.',
 p:['<b>Carvone</b> comes in two enantiomers. (R)-carvone smells of spearmint, and (S)-carvone smells of caraway seeds, the flavour in rye bread.',
 'The two molecules have identical boiling points, densities and IR spectra. In a non-chiral environment they behave identically. The only physical difference is that they rotate plane-polarised light in opposite directions.',
 'Your nose is not a non-chiral environment. Odour receptors are proteins built from L-amino acids, so they are themselves chiral, and a receptor can bind one enantiomer better than its mirror image, just as your right hand fits a right glove.',
 'Limonene is a similar pair: (R)-limonene smells of oranges, while (S)-limonene has a harsher, more turpentine-like smell.'],
 x:'Chirality; why enantiomers differ only in chiral environments.',k:'enantiomers, receptors, odour'},
{s:'why-ice-floats',c:'Water',t:'Why ice floats, and why it matters',
 h:'Almost every solid sinks in its own liquid. Water is a rare exception.',
 p:['Liquid water is densest at about 4 °C. When it freezes, each molecule hydrogen-bonds to four neighbours in a rigid tetrahedral network with a lot of empty space, so ice is about 9% less dense than liquid water.',
 'That is why ice floats, why pipes burst in winter, and why lakes freeze from the top down. The floating ice insulates the water beneath, which stays near 4 °C at the bottom, so fish survive the winter.',
 'If ice sank, lakes and oceans in cold regions would freeze solid from the bottom up, and Earth’s climate and life would look very different.',
 'The same hydrogen bonds explain water’s unusually high boiling point for such a small molecule: compare H<sub>2</sub>O (100 °C) with H<sub>2</sub>S (about −60 °C).'],
 x:'Hydrogen bonding and the anomalous properties of water.',k:'hydrogen bonding, density, ice'},
{s:'gallium-melts-in-your-hand',c:'Elements',t:'The metal that melts in your hand',
 h:'Gallium is solid on your desk and liquid in your palm.',
 p:['Gallium melts at 29.8 °C, below body temperature, so a piece held in your hand slowly turns into a silvery puddle. Yet it does not boil until about 2400 °C, one of the widest liquid ranges of any element.',
 'Its solid structure is unusual for a metal: atoms pair up into Ga<sub>2</sub> units with partly covalent bonding. The solid is less ordered in the metallic sense, so it takes little energy to melt.',
 'Like water, gallium expands when it freezes, so it is shipped in plastic rather than glass.',
 'It is also hard on other metals: liquid gallium seeps between the grains of aluminium and makes it crumble. Most gallium today ends up in semiconductors such as gallium nitride, used in blue LEDs and fast phone chargers.'],
 x:'Metallic bonding; structure and melting point.',k:'metals, melting points, semiconductors'},
{s:'teflon-accident',c:'Discovery',t:'Teflon was discovered when a gas cylinder went quiet',
 h:'A chemist opened an "empty" cylinder and found a slippery white powder.',
 p:['In 1938 Roy Plunkett at DuPont was working with tetrafluoroethene gas for refrigerants. One morning a cylinder that should have been full gave out no gas, yet it still weighed as much as a full one.',
 'He cut it open and found a waxy white solid. The tetrafluoroethene had polymerised inside the cylinder, catalysed by the iron of the walls, into <b>poly(tetrafluoroethene)</b>, PTFE.',
 'PTFE is a carbon chain completely sheathed in fluorine atoms. C–F bonds are among the strongest single bonds in organic chemistry, and the tightly held fluorine electrons give very weak London forces, so almost nothing sticks to it and almost nothing reacts with it.',
 'The same stability is why some related fluorinated chemicals (PFAS) persist in the environment for so long.'],
 x:'Addition polymerisation; bond strength and polymer disposal.',k:'polymers, C–F bond, PFAS'},
{s:'perkins-purple',c:'History',t:'An 18-year-old invented purple by accident',
 h:'He was trying to make a malaria drug and made fashion history instead.',
 p:['In 1856 William Perkin, an 18-year-old student, was trying to make quinine, the antimalarial drug, from coal-tar chemicals during his Easter holiday. His reaction with aniline produced a black sludge.',
 'When he washed it with alcohol, he got a brilliant purple solution. He had made <b>mauveine</b>, the first commercially successful synthetic dye.',
 'Purple dyes had been extremely expensive, because the ancient dye Tyrian purple came from thousands of sea snails. Perkin’s colour started a craze, and he became rich by his early twenties.',
 'His accident launched the synthetic dye industry, which in turn grew into the modern chemical and pharmaceutical industries. Azo dyes, which you meet in A Level and JEE organic chemistry, are part of that family.'],
 x:'Aromatic amines and azo dyes.',k:'dyes, aniline, industry'},
{s:'haber-bosch',c:'Industry',t:'About half the nitrogen in your body passed through a factory',
 h:'One reaction feeds roughly half of humanity.',
 p:['Plants need nitrogen, and the air is 78% nitrogen, but N<sub>2</sub> is almost inert because of its very strong triple bond. Before 1913, farmers relied on manure, guano and nitrate mines.',
 'Fritz Haber found how to combine nitrogen and hydrogen into ammonia, and Carl Bosch scaled it up: N<sub>2</sub> + 3H<sub>2</sub> ⇌ 2NH<sub>3</sub>, at about 200 atmospheres and 400–450 °C over an iron catalyst.',
 'The conditions are a compromise. Low temperature gives a better yield because the reaction is exothermic, but it would be far too slow, so a moderate temperature and a catalyst give a reasonable yield quickly. High pressure favours the side with fewer gas molecules.',
 'Ammonia fertilisers now support food for about half of the world’s population, so a large share of the nitrogen atoms in your proteins were fixed by the Haber process.'],
 x:'Le Chatelier’s principle and industrial equilibria.',k:'equilibrium, catalysts, ammonia'},
{s:'kekule-dream',c:'History',t:'Did a dream of a snake reveal benzene?',
 h:'The most famous story in chemistry may be just that: a story.',
 p:['Benzene, C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>6</sub>, puzzled chemists. It has as few hydrogens as a highly unsaturated compound, yet it does not react like an alkene.',
 'In 1865 August Kekulé proposed a ring of six carbons with alternating single and double bonds. Twenty-five years later, at a celebration in his honour, he said the idea came from a daydream of a snake biting its own tail.',
 'Historians are not sure it happened that way, and other chemists had suggested rings before. Either way, Kekulé’s structure was not the final answer: benzene does not flip between two forms. All six C–C bonds are the same length, 140 pm, between a single and a double bond.',
 'The modern picture is a ring of six sp² carbons with a delocalised π system above and below the ring. That delocalisation makes benzene about 150 kJ mol⁻¹ more stable than Kekulé’s structure predicts, which is why it prefers substitution to addition.'],
 x:'Structure of benzene, delocalisation and aromatic stability.',k:'benzene, delocalisation, history'},
{s:'superglue',c:'Everyday',t:'Super glue sets by reacting with water',
 h:'The moisture on your fingers is exactly what makes it stick.',
 p:['Super glue is mostly a cyanoacrylate, such as ethyl 2-cyanoacrylate. Its C=C bond carries two strongly electron-withdrawing groups, a nitrile and an ester.',
 'Those groups make the alkene carbon very electrophilic. Even a weak nucleophile, such as the hydroxide in traces of water on a surface, can attack it. That starts anionic polymerisation: each new carbanion attacks another monomer, and long chains form in seconds.',
 'That is why the glue stays liquid in a sealed tube but bonds skin almost instantly.',
 'It was discovered in 1942 by Harry Coover, who was trying to make clear plastic gun sights and rejected it for being too sticky. He found a use for it years later. Forensic scientists use cyanoacrylate fumes to reveal fingerprints, because the polymer grows on the moisture in the print.'],
 x:'Addition polymerisation; electrophiles and nucleophiles.',k:'polymerisation, nucleophiles, adhesives'},
{s:'chilli-heat',c:'Food',t:'Why milk, not water, cools a chilli burn',
 h:'Chilli does not burn you. It tricks your heat sensors.',
 p:['The heat of chilli comes from <b>capsaicin</b>. It binds to a receptor called TRPV1 on pain-sensing nerves, the same receptor that responds to temperatures above about 43 °C. Your brain reads the signal as heat.',
 'Capsaicin is mostly non-polar: a long hydrocarbon tail attached to a phenol and an amide. It dissolves poorly in water, so drinking water just spreads it around your mouth.',
 'Milk works better. Its fat dissolves capsaicin, and the protein casein helps lift it away from the receptors. A fatty or oily food has a similar effect.',
 'Chilli heat is measured in Scoville heat units. Pure capsaicin is about 16 million. Birds lack the version of TRPV1 that capsaicin activates, so they eat chillies happily and spread the seeds.'],
 x:'Polarity and solubility: like dissolves like.',k:'solubility, polarity, receptors'},
{s:'autumn-leaves',c:'Nature',t:'Autumn colours were in the leaf all summer',
 h:'Yellow leaves are not turning yellow. The green is leaving.',
 p:['Leaves are green because chlorophyll absorbs red and blue light strongly and reflects green. Chlorophyll is constantly broken down and rebuilt in sunlight.',
 'As autumn days shorten, trees stop making chlorophyll and recycle its nitrogen. As the green fades, the yellow and orange <b>carotenoids</b> that were there all along become visible.',
 'Reds and purples are different: they come from <b>anthocyanins</b>, which many trees make freshly in autumn. Their colour depends on pH, which is why the same tree can look redder or more purple from year to year.',
 'The colour of each pigment comes from its long chain of conjugated double bonds. The longer the conjugation, the lower the energy gap, and the longer the wavelength of light it absorbs.'],
 x:'Absorption of light and colour; conjugation.',k:'pigments, conjugation, light absorption'},
{s:'statue-of-liberty-green',c:'Metals',t:'The Statue of Liberty used to be copper-coloured',
 h:'She turned green in about 30 years, and that green protects her.',
 p:['When it was unveiled in 1886, the statue was the shiny reddish-brown of new copper. Within about 30 years it had turned blue-green.',
 'Copper slowly reacts with oxygen, water, carbon dioxide and sulfur compounds in the air. First a layer of copper oxides forms, then green basic copper salts, mainly copper carbonates and sulfates (and chlorides near the sea).',
 'This layer, the <b>patina</b>, is not like rust on iron. It sticks firmly and seals the metal underneath, so the copper skin, only about 2.4 mm thick, has lasted well over a century.',
 'Rust on iron flakes off and exposes fresh metal, which is why iron needs paint, galvanising or sacrificial zinc to protect it.'],
 x:'Redox reactions of metals; corrosion.',k:'oxidation, corrosion, copper'},
{s:'glow-sticks',c:'Light',t:'How a glow stick makes light without heat',
 h:'Bend it and you start a reaction that releases energy as light.',
 p:['A glow stick has two compartments. The outer tube holds a phenyl oxalate ester and a fluorescent dye; a thin glass vial inside holds hydrogen peroxide. Bending the stick breaks the vial and mixes them.',
 'The peroxide reacts with the oxalate ester to make a high-energy intermediate, thought to be 1,2-dioxetanedione, which breaks down into carbon dioxide. Instead of releasing its energy as heat, it passes it to the dye molecule, lifting an electron to an excited state.',
 'When the electron falls back down, the dye emits a photon. The dye decides the colour.',
 'This is <b>chemiluminescence</b>. Putting a glow stick in the freezer slows the reaction, so it glows dimmer but lasts longer; warm water makes it brighter but shorter-lived.'],
 x:'Effect of temperature on rate; energy levels and emission.',k:'kinetics, light, excited states'},
{s:'firework-colours',c:'Light',t:'The chemistry of firework colours',
 h:'Every colour in the sky is a metal’s fingerprint.',
 p:['Firework colours come from metal salts heated in the explosion. Heat excites electrons to higher energy levels, and as they fall back they emit light at wavelengths set by the energy gaps.',
 'Strontium salts give red, sodium gives intense yellow-orange, barium gives green, and copper compounds give blue. Lithium gives a crimson red and calcium an orange.',
 'Blue is the hardest. The best blue comes from copper(I) chloride species, which break down if the flame is too hot, so pyrotechnicians balance the temperature carefully.',
 'This is the same physics as the flame test in the lab: dip a wire in a salt and hold it in a Bunsen flame, and the colour identifies the metal ion.'],
 x:'Flame tests and emission spectra.',k:'emission, flame tests, metals'},
{s:'aspirin-willow',c:'Medicine',t:'Aspirin started as willow bark',
 h:'People chewed willow bark for pain for thousands of years.',
 p:['Willow bark contains salicin, which the body converts to salicylic acid, a mild painkiller and fever reducer. Salicylic acid itself works, but it irritates the stomach.',
 'In 1897, chemists at Bayer, notably Felix Hoffmann, made a gentler version by turning its phenol group into an ester with ethanoic anhydride: <b>acetylsalicylic acid</b>, sold as aspirin from 1899.',
 'Aspirin works by permanently transferring that acetyl group onto an enzyme, cyclooxygenase, blocking the production of prostaglandins that cause pain and inflammation. It also makes blood platelets less sticky, which is why low doses are used to prevent some heart attacks.',
 'The synthesis of aspirin from salicylic acid is a classic school experiment in esterification and purification by recrystallisation.'],
 x:'Esters, phenols and acylation.',k:'esters, phenols, drugs'},
{s:'penicillin',c:'Medicine',t:'Penicillin works because its ring is under strain',
 h:'A four-membered ring is the loaded spring that kills bacteria.',
 p:['In 1928 Alexander Fleming noticed that a mould contaminating one of his bacterial plates had killed the bacteria around it. The active compound was penicillin.',
 'At its heart is a <b>β-lactam</b>: a four-membered ring containing an amide. The bond angles in the ring are forced to about 90°, far from the normal angles, and the amide cannot share its lone pair as it normally would. Both make the carbonyl unusually reactive.',
 'Bacteria build their cell walls with enzymes that cross-link the wall. Penicillin fits the enzyme’s active site, and its strained ring opens and acylates the enzyme, switching it off. Without a strong wall, the bacteria burst.',
 'Resistant bacteria make enzymes called β-lactamases that open the ring before it reaches its target, one reason doctors ask you to finish the course of antibiotics.'],
 x:'Amides and ring strain; enzymes and inhibitors.',k:'amides, ring strain, antibiotics'},
{s:'graphene-scotch-tape',c:'Materials',t:'A Nobel Prize won with sticky tape',
 h:'The world’s thinnest material was peeled off a pencil lead.',
 p:['Graphite is made of flat sheets of carbon atoms in hexagons, stacked like pages and held together only by weak London forces. A single sheet is called <b>graphene</b>.',
 'In 2004 Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov in Manchester stuck tape to graphite, peeled it off, and kept peeling the flakes on the tape until some were one atom thick. They won the 2010 Nobel Prize in Physics.',
 'Graphene is one atom thick yet very strong, because every carbon is held by three strong σ bonds in the sheet. Each carbon also contributes one electron to a delocalised π system, so graphene conducts electricity extremely well.',
 'The same structure explains why graphite is soft and slippery (sheets slide) but has a very high melting point (the sheets themselves are giant covalent structures).'],
 x:'Giant covalent structures: graphite, diamond and graphene.',k:'carbon allotropes, bonding, structure'},
{s:'diamonds-arent-forever',c:'Materials',t:'Diamonds are not forever',
 h:'At room temperature, graphite is the more stable form of carbon.',
 p:['Under everyday conditions, graphite is thermodynamically more stable than diamond by a couple of kJ per mole. So, strictly, every diamond is slowly "trying" to become graphite.',
 'It never gets there on any human timescale. Converting diamond to graphite would mean breaking strong C–C bonds in a rigid 3D network, so the activation energy is enormous and the rate is effectively zero.',
 'This is the difference between thermodynamics and kinetics. Thermodynamics says which way a change could go; kinetics says how fast. Diamond is <b>metastable</b>: stable in practice, not in principle.',
 'Heat a diamond strongly without air and it will turn to graphite. Heat it in air and it burns to carbon dioxide like any other carbon.'],
 x:'Thermodynamic versus kinetic stability; activation energy.',k:'thermodynamics, kinetics, carbon'},
{s:'buckyballs',c:'Materials',t:'A football made of 60 carbon atoms',
 h:'A third form of carbon was found in 1985, shaped like a football.',
 p:['In 1985 Harold Kroto, Robert Curl and Richard Smalley vaporised graphite with a laser and found a surprisingly strong signal at 720 in the mass spectrum: a cluster of exactly 60 carbon atoms.',
 'They proposed a hollow cage of 20 hexagons and 12 pentagons, the pattern of a football, and named it <b>buckminsterfullerene</b> after the architect Buckminster Fuller, famous for geodesic domes. They shared the 1996 Nobel Prize in Chemistry.',
 'C<sub>60</sub> is a simple molecular solid: each cage is a separate molecule held to the next by London forces. That is why it dissolves in some organic solvents, unlike diamond or graphite.',
 'The discovery opened the way to carbon nanotubes and the wider family of fullerenes.'],
 x:'Simple molecular lattices (C60) and mass spectrometry.',k:'fullerenes, carbon, structure'},
{s:'noble-gas-compounds',c:'Elements',t:'The day the noble gases stopped being noble',
 h:'For decades, textbooks said they formed no compounds at all.',
 p:['Helium, neon, argon, krypton, xenon and radon were called the inert gases: with full outer shells, they were thought to be unable to bond.',
 'In 1962 Neil Bartlett noticed that platinum hexafluoride was strong enough to remove an electron from oxygen gas. The first ionisation energy of xenon is almost the same as that of O<sub>2</sub>, so he mixed xenon with PtF<sub>6</sub>, and an orange-yellow solid formed: the first noble gas compound.',
 'Simple xenon fluorides followed quickly, such as XeF<sub>2</sub>, XeF<sub>4</sub> and XeF<sub>6</sub>. Their shapes are classic VSEPR examples: XeF<sub>4</sub> is square planar, because xenon has two lone pairs as well as four bonding pairs.',
 'Xenon reacts because it is large: its outer electrons are far from the nucleus and well shielded, so it has the lowest ionisation energy of the stable noble gases.'],
 x:'Ionisation energy trends; VSEPR with lone pairs.',k:'noble gases, VSEPR, ionisation energy'},
{s:'fluorine-martyrs',c:'History',t:'The element that fought back',
 h:'Isolating fluorine took more than 70 years and injured several chemists.',
 p:['Fluorine is the most electronegative element and the most reactive non-metal. It attacks glass, most metals and water, and it is extremely toxic.',
 'From the early 1800s chemists knew fluorides contained a new element, but every attempt to free it failed, because fluorine immediately reacts with whatever it is made in. Several chemists were badly poisoned trying; they are sometimes called the "fluorine martyrs".',
 'In 1886 Henri Moissan succeeded by electrolysing a solution of potassium hydrogen fluoride in liquid hydrogen fluoride at low temperature, in an apparatus made of platinum. He won the 1906 Nobel Prize.',
 'Fluorine is so reactive partly because the F–F bond is unusually weak for a small atom: the lone pairs on the two close atoms repel each other.'],
 x:'Halogens: electronegativity, oxidising power and bond strength.',k:'halogens, electrolysis, reactivity'},
{s:'ozone-hole',c:'Environment',t:'How one chlorine atom destroys thousands of ozone molecules',
 h:'The ozone hole is a chain reaction in the sky.',
 p:['High in the stratosphere, ozone (O<sub>3</sub>) absorbs much of the Sun’s harmful UV-B radiation. In the 1970s Mario Molina and Sherwood Rowland showed that chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs), then used in sprays and fridges, could destroy it.',
 'CFCs are so unreactive that they survive long enough to drift up to the stratosphere. There, UV light breaks a C–Cl bond homolytically, releasing chlorine atoms.',
 'Each chlorine radical runs a catalytic cycle: Cl + O<sub>3</sub> → ClO + O<sub>2</sub>, then ClO + O → Cl + O<sub>2</sub>. The chlorine comes out unchanged, ready to go again, so a single atom can destroy many thousands of ozone molecules.',
 'The 1987 Montreal Protocol phased out CFCs, and the ozone layer is expected to recover by around the middle of this century. Molina, Rowland and Paul Crutzen shared the 1995 Nobel Prize.'],
 x:'Free radicals, chain reactions and homogeneous catalysis.',k:'radicals, catalysis, environment'},
{s:'maillard-reaction',c:'Food',t:'Toast, coffee and steak share one reaction',
 h:'The browning that makes food taste cooked is a reaction between sugars and amino acids.',
 p:['When you toast bread, sear meat or roast coffee, a reducing sugar reacts with an amino acid. The carbonyl group of the sugar condenses with the amino group, losing water, and the product goes on through a cascade of rearrangements.',
 'This is the <b>Maillard reaction</b>, named after Louis-Camille Maillard, who described it in 1912. It produces hundreds of different flavour and aroma molecules and brown pigments called melanoidins.',
 'It needs heat and goes fastest when the surface is fairly dry, which is why you pat meat dry before searing it and why boiled meat does not brown: the surface cannot get much above 100 °C while water is there.',
 'Caramelisation is different: it is sugar breaking down on its own, at higher temperatures, with no amino acids involved.'],
 x:'Condensation reactions of carbonyls with amines.',k:'carbonyls, amines, condensation'},
{s:'baking-soda-powder',c:'Food',t:'Baking soda and baking powder are not the same',
 h:'One needs an acid from your recipe; the other brings its own.',
 p:['Both make cakes rise by releasing carbon dioxide bubbles. <b>Baking soda</b> is sodium hydrogencarbonate, NaHCO<sub>3</sub>. It needs an acid to react: HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> + H<sup>+</sup> → H<sub>2</sub>O + CO<sub>2</sub>. Recipes with baking soda include an acidic ingredient such as buttermilk, yoghurt, lemon juice or brown sugar.',
 '<b>Baking powder</b> is baking soda already mixed with a dry acid salt, such as monocalcium phosphate or sodium acid pyrophosphate, plus starch to keep it dry. Add water and the two react.',
 '"Double-acting" baking powder contains two acids: one reacts as soon as the batter is wet, the other only in the hot oven, giving a second rise.',
 'Baking soda used without enough acid leaves sodium carbonate behind, which tastes soapy and bitter.'],
 x:'Reactions of acids with hydrogencarbonates.',k:'acids, carbonates, gases'},
{s:'how-soap-works',c:'Everyday',t:'How soap grabs grease',
 h:'Each soap molecule has a water-loving head and a grease-loving tail.',
 p:['A soap molecule is the sodium or potassium salt of a long-chain fatty acid. One end is an ionic carboxylate group, which interacts strongly with water. The other end is a long hydrocarbon chain that does not.',
 'In water, soap molecules cluster into <b>micelles</b>: spheres with the tails pointing inwards and the charged heads facing the water. Grease and oil dissolve in the non-polar core.',
 'When you rinse, the micelles carry the trapped grease away in the water. This is also how soap breaks apart the fatty membrane of many viruses.',
 'Soap is made by heating fats with sodium hydroxide, hydrolysing the ester bonds in triglycerides. The reaction is called saponification, from the Latin for soap.'],
 x:'Ester hydrolysis; polar and non-polar interactions.',k:'esters, micelles, hydrolysis'},
{s:'why-ice-is-slippery',c:'Water',t:'Nobody fully agrees on why ice is slippery',
 h:'The textbook answer is probably wrong.',
 p:['For a long time, the standard explanation was that the pressure of a skate blade melts the ice. The numbers do not work: the pressure under a skater lowers the melting point by only a fraction of a degree, yet ice is slippery at −30 °C.',
 'A second idea is frictional heating: sliding generates heat that melts a thin film of water. That helps once you are moving, but it does not explain why stationary ice is slippery too.',
 'The most widely supported picture today is a <b>quasi-liquid layer</b>. Molecules at the surface of ice have fewer neighbours to hydrogen-bond with, so the top few layers are more disordered and mobile than the crystal below, even well below 0 °C.',
 'Recent studies suggest all three effects contribute, in proportions that depend on temperature and speed. It is a reminder that some everyday chemistry is still active research.'],
 x:'Hydrogen bonding; surfaces and phase changes.',k:'ice, hydrogen bonding, research'},
{s:'supercooled-water',c:'Water',t:'Water can stay liquid below 0 °C',
 h:'Very pure water can be cooled well below its freezing point, then freeze in a flash.',
 p:['Freezing needs a starting point. Ice crystals form around a <b>nucleus</b>: a dust particle, a scratch on the container or a tiny ice crystal. In very pure, still water there is nothing to start on.',
 'So water can be cooled below 0 °C and stay liquid. It is <b>supercooled</b>, a metastable state. Small droplets in clouds are often supercooled to −20 °C or lower.',
 'Tap a bottle of supercooled water, or drop in an ice crystal, and freezing spreads through it in seconds. As it freezes it releases its latent heat, warming itself back to 0 °C, so only part of it turns to ice.',
 'Freezing rain happens when supercooled droplets hit cold surfaces and freeze on contact, coating roads and power lines in ice.'],
 x:'Phase changes and enthalpy of fusion.',k:'phase changes, nucleation, enthalpy'},
{s:'aerogel',c:'Materials',t:'The solid that is almost all air',
 h:'Some aerogels are more than 99% air yet can hold up a brick.',
 p:['An aerogel starts as a gel: a sponge-like network of silica (SiO<sub>2</sub>) particles filled with liquid. If you simply let the liquid evaporate, surface tension pulls the delicate network together and it collapses.',
 'The trick is supercritical drying. Above its critical point a fluid has no liquid–gas boundary and no surface tension, so it can be removed without crushing the network. What is left is a solid that is mostly empty space.',
 'Silica aerogels are among the lightest solids ever made and are superb thermal insulators, because heat struggles to pass through the tangled network and the tiny pores.',
 'NASA used aerogel on the Stardust mission to catch comet dust gently at several kilometres per second.'],
 x:'Giant covalent structures (SiO₂); phase diagrams and critical points.',k:'materials, silica, phase diagrams'},
{s:'lithium-ion-battery',c:'Energy',t:'Inside the battery in your phone',
 h:'Lithium ions shuttle between two electrodes every time you charge.',
 p:['A lithium-ion cell has a graphite anode and a cathode made of a metal oxide, such as lithium cobalt oxide. Lithium ions sit between the layers of these materials, a process called intercalation.',
 'When the phone runs, lithium atoms in the graphite give up electrons, which flow through the circuit, while the Li<sup>+</sup> ions travel through the electrolyte to the cathode. Charging pushes them back.',
 'Lithium is ideal: it is the lightest metal and has a very negative electrode potential, so cells are light and give a high voltage, about 3.7 V each.',
 'John Goodenough, Stanley Whittingham and Akira Yoshino shared the 2019 Nobel Prize in Chemistry for developing it. Goodenough was 97, the oldest Nobel laureate ever.'],
 x:'Electrochemical cells and electrode potentials.',k:'electrochemistry, redox, batteries'},
{s:'avogadro-exact',c:'Numbers',t:'Avogadro’s number became exact in 2019',
 h:'The mole used to be defined by a lump of carbon. Now it is just a number.',
 p:['Until 2019, one mole was defined as the number of atoms in exactly 12 g of carbon-12, so the Avogadro constant was something you measured, with an uncertainty.',
 'Since 20 May 2019, the mole is defined the other way round: one mole contains exactly <b>6.022 140 76 × 10<sup>23</sup></b> particles. The number is fixed by definition.',
 'The same revision redefined the kilogram using the Planck constant, retiring the platinum–iridium cylinder in Paris that had defined it since 1889.',
 'How big is a mole? A mole of grains of rice would cover the land area of Earth many metres deep. A mole of water, though, is just 18 g, about a tablespoon, because atoms are so small.'],
 x:'The mole and the Avogadro constant.',k:'mole, Avogadro constant, SI units'},
{s:'newest-elements',c:'Elements',t:'The four newest elements',
 h:'The periodic table’s seventh row was completed in 2016.',
 p:['In 2016 IUPAC approved names for elements 113, 115, 117 and 118: <b>nihonium</b> (Nh, after Japan), <b>moscovium</b> (Mc, after Moscow), <b>tennessine</b> (Ts, after Tennessee) and <b>oganesson</b> (Og, after the physicist Yuri Oganessian).',
 'They were made by firing beams of lighter nuclei into heavy targets in particle accelerators. Only a handful of atoms of each have ever been made, and they decay within fractions of a second.',
 'Oganessian was only the second person to have an element named after him while still alive, after Glenn Seaborg (seaborgium).',
 'Element 119 would start an eighth row. Teams in several countries are trying to make it, but the chance of success in each collision is tiny.'],
 x:'Structure of the periodic table; nucleons and isotopes.',k:'periodic table, nuclear chemistry, elements'},
{s:'technetium',c:'Elements',t:'The first element made by humans',
 h:'There is a gap in the middle of the transition metals that nature barely fills.',
 p:['Element 43 was predicted by Mendeleev but stubbornly missing from nature. In 1937 Emilio Segrè and Carlo Perrier found it in a piece of molybdenum that had been bombarded in a cyclotron. They called it <b>technetium</b>, from the Greek for "artificial".',
 'Technetium has no stable isotopes at all, so any that existed when Earth formed has long since decayed. Only traces form naturally, from uranium fission.',
 'One isotope, technetium-99m, is now used in tens of millions of medical scans a year. It emits gamma rays that a camera outside the body can detect, and its half-life of about six hours is long enough for a scan but short enough to fade quickly.',
 'Doctors attach it to different molecules to target bones, the heart or other organs.'],
 x:'Isotopes, radioactive decay and half-life.',k:'isotopes, radioactivity, medicine'},
{s:'radioactive-bananas',c:'Everyday',t:'Bananas are slightly radioactive',
 h:'So are you, for exactly the same reason.',
 p:['Bananas are rich in potassium, and a small fraction (about 0.012%) of all potassium is the radioactive isotope <b>potassium-40</b>, with a half-life of about 1.25 billion years.',
 'A typical banana contains enough potassium-40 for about fifteen radioactive decays every second. The extra dose from eating one is tiny, and your body keeps its potassium level roughly constant, so eating more bananas does not build up radioactivity.',
 'Your own body has about 140 g of potassium, so several thousand potassium-40 atoms decay inside you every second. Carbon-14 in your body adds a similar amount.',
 'Potassium-40 slowly decays to argon-40, which is why argon is the third most common gas in the air and why geologists can date volcanic rocks by measuring trapped argon.'],
 x:'Isotopes and radioactive decay.',k:'isotopes, half-life, potassium'},
{s:'carbon-dating',c:'Methods',t:'How carbon-14 tells the age of a bone',
 h:'Every living thing carries a clock that starts ticking when it dies.',
 p:['Cosmic rays in the upper atmosphere turn nitrogen-14 into <b>carbon-14</b>, which ends up in carbon dioxide. Plants take it in, animals eat the plants, so every living thing has about the same tiny ratio of carbon-14 to carbon-12.',
 'When an organism dies it stops taking in carbon. Its carbon-14 decays with a half-life of about 5730 years and is not replaced.',
 'Measure how much carbon-14 is left compared with a living sample, and you can calculate how long ago it died: after one half-life, half remains; after two, a quarter.',
 'The method, developed by Willard Libby (Nobel Prize 1960), works back to about 50 000 years. Beyond that, too little carbon-14 is left to measure. Modern labs count the carbon-14 atoms directly with a mass spectrometer.'],
 x:'First-order kinetics and half-life.',k:'half-life, isotopes, first order'},
{s:'chirality-of-life',c:'Biology',t:'Life is left-handed',
 h:'Almost all the amino acids in your proteins have the same handedness.',
 p:['Every amino acid except glycine has a chiral carbon, so it could exist as two mirror images, labelled L and D. Laboratory syntheses without a chiral influence make both equally.',
 'Living things, though, build their proteins almost entirely from <b>L-amino acids</b>, and the sugars they make, such as glucose and the ribose in RNA, are almost all D.',
 'This "homochirality" matters: a protein built from a mix of L and D amino acids would not fold into a consistent shape, and enzymes, being chiral, usually work with only one enantiomer of their substrate.',
 'Why life picked L rather than D is still an open question. Ideas include a small excess from polarised light in space or meteorites, amplified by chemistry on the early Earth.'],
 x:'Chirality; amino acids and proteins.',k:'chirality, amino acids, origin of life'},
{s:'cut-grass-smell',c:'Smell',t:'The smell of cut grass is a distress signal',
 h:'That fresh, green smell is a plant calling for help.',
 p:['When you mow a lawn, damaged leaf cells release enzymes that break down fatty acids in their membranes. Within seconds they produce small molecules called green leaf volatiles.',
 'One of the first is <b>(Z)-hex-3-enal</b>, a six-carbon aldehyde with a cis double bond. It is quickly converted to (Z)-hex-3-en-1-ol, known as "leaf alcohol", which is used in perfumes and flavourings to give a green note.',
 'For the plant, these molecules help seal wounds and fight microbes, and in some species they attract predators of the insects eating the leaves.',
 'The cis geometry matters to the smell: E/Z isomers of the same molecule often smell quite different.'],
 x:'Aldehydes, alcohols and E/Z isomerism.',k:'geometric isomers, aldehydes, odour'},
{s:'vanillin',c:'Food',t:'Most vanilla flavour never saw a vanilla orchid',
 h:'Real vanilla is the second most expensive spice in the world, after saffron.',
 p:['The main flavour molecule in vanilla is <b>vanillin</b>: a benzene ring carrying an aldehyde, a methoxy group and a phenol OH.',
 'Natural vanilla comes from the cured pods of an orchid, and each flower must usually be pollinated by hand. Demand is far greater than supply, so the vast majority of vanillin sold is made in factories.',
 'Most synthetic vanillin is made from guaiacol, a petrochemical, and some from lignin, the tough polymer in wood, a by-product of paper making. The molecule is identical either way.',
 'Real vanilla tastes richer because the pods contain hundreds of other compounds as well. Vanillin also has a famous cousin: ethyl vanillin, with an ethoxy instead of a methoxy group, is about three times stronger.'],
 x:'Functional groups on benzene: aldehydes, ethers and phenols.',k:'aromatic compounds, aldehydes, flavour'},
{s:'caffeine',c:'Medicine',t:'Caffeine does not give you energy',
 h:'It blocks the molecule that tells your brain you are tired.',
 p:['While you are awake, a molecule called <b>adenosine</b> builds up in your brain and binds to receptors that make you feel sleepy.',
 'Caffeine has a similar shape to the purine part of adenosine: both are built on a purine skeleton, two fused rings containing nitrogen. Caffeine fits into adenosine receptors but does not activate them, so it blocks the "tired" signal. Chemists call that an antagonist.',
 'When caffeine wears off, the adenosine that has been building up can bind again, which is part of the afternoon crash.',
 'Caffeine is a natural insecticide made by coffee, tea and cacao plants. Some decaffeinated coffee is made by extracting the caffeine with supercritical carbon dioxide.'],
 x:'Heterocycles and amines; shape and binding.',k:'receptors, heterocycles, drugs'},
{s:'apples-turn-brown',c:'Food',t:'Why cut apples turn brown',
 h:'It is an enzyme reaction you can stop with lemon juice.',
 p:['Cutting an apple breaks cells and lets an enzyme, <b>polyphenol oxidase</b>, meet phenol compounds and oxygen from the air.',
 'The enzyme oxidises the phenols to quinones. Quinones are reactive and link together into brown polymers, similar to the melanin pigments in skin.',
 'Lemon juice slows the browning in two ways: its low pH slows the enzyme, and its vitamin C (ascorbic acid) is a reducing agent that turns quinones back into phenols. Keeping fruit cold, or blanching it to denature the enzyme, also helps.',
 'The same reaction gives tea, coffee and raisins their brown colours, so it is not always unwanted.'],
 x:'Oxidation of phenols; enzymes and pH.',k:'oxidation, enzymes, phenols'},
{s:'smell-of-the-sea',c:'Smell',t:'The smell of the sea comes from plankton',
 h:'That seaside smell is a sulfur compound that also helps make clouds.',
 p:['Tiny marine algae make a compound called DMSP to help them cope with salty water. When they are eaten or die, bacteria break it down into <b>dimethyl sulfide</b>, (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>S.',
 'At high concentrations dimethyl sulfide smells like cooked cabbage, but at the low levels near the sea it gives the familiar fresh seaside smell. Seabirds use it to find areas rich in plankton, and so food.',
 'Once in the air, dimethyl sulfide is oxidised to sulfate particles, which act as seeds for water droplets in clouds.',
 'So plankton may influence the weather: more plankton, more dimethyl sulfide, more cloud seeds, a link that climate scientists are still measuring.'],
 x:'Sulfur compounds and oxidation in the atmosphere.',k:'sulfur, atmosphere, oxidation'},
{s:'durian',c:'Food',t:'Why durian smells the way it does',
 h:'The king of fruits owes its smell to a mix of sweet esters and sulfur compounds.',
 p:['Durian is famous in Southeast Asia for a smell so strong that many hotels and trains ban it. Chemists who analysed its aroma found dozens of odour-active compounds working together.',
 'Fruity, sweet notes come from <b>esters</b>, the same family that gives pineapples and bananas their smells. The onion-like, rotten-egg notes come from <b>sulfur compounds</b>, especially thiols, which contain an –SH group.',
 'Thiols are among the most smelly molecules known. Your nose detects some of them at parts per trillion, which is why gas companies add a thiol to odourless natural gas so leaks can be smelled.',
 'Whether durian smells heavenly or awful depends a lot on which notes your nose picks out, and on what you grew up eating.'],
 x:'Esters and thiols; functional groups and odour.',k:'esters, thiols, odour'},
{s:'pufferfish-toxin',c:'Nature',t:'The toxin that blocks your nerves',
 h:'Pufferfish carry a molecule that plugs a channel in nerve cells.',
 p:['Some pufferfish, served as fugu in Japan by specially licensed chefs, contain <b>tetrodotoxin</b>. The fish do not make it themselves: it comes from bacteria they live with or eat.',
 'Nerves send signals by letting sodium ions rush into the cell through protein channels. Tetrodotoxin has a positively charged guanidinium group that fits the mouth of the sodium channel like a cork in a bottle, so the signal cannot pass.',
 'That is why it causes numbness and paralysis. There is no antidote; treatment is support, such as helping patients breathe until the toxin clears.',
 'Because it blocks sodium channels so precisely, tetrodotoxin is a valuable research tool in neuroscience, and it is being studied as a painkiller at very low doses.'],
 x:'Ions, charges and molecular shape in binding.',k:'toxins, ions, biochemistry'},
{s:'vulcanised-rubber',c:'Materials',t:'Rubber was useless until someone added sulfur',
 h:'Early rubber turned sticky in summer and brittle in winter.',
 p:['Natural rubber is a polymer of isoprene, poly(cis-isoprene), with long chains that slide past each other. In heat it goes soft and sticky; in cold it cracks.',
 'In 1839 Charles Goodyear found that heating rubber with sulfur transformed it. The sulfur forms <b>cross-links</b>, short bridges of sulfur atoms between neighbouring chains.',
 'Now the chains can stretch and uncoil, but they cannot slide past each other permanently, so the rubber snaps back to its shape. More cross-links give a harder material; car tyres use carefully controlled amounts.',
 'Cross-linking is why some polymers are thermosets, which cannot be remelted, rather than thermoplastics, which can.'],
 x:'Polymers: chain structure and properties.',k:'polymers, cross-linking, alkenes'},
{s:'nylon',c:'Materials',t:'Nylon: the polymer that replaced silk',
 h:'When nylon stockings went on sale in 1940, shops sold out in hours.',
 p:['In 1935 Wallace Carothers’s team at DuPont made <b>nylon-6,6</b> from hexane-1,6-diamine and hexanedioic acid. The amine group of one monomer reacts with the acid group of another to form an amide bond, releasing water.',
 'This is condensation polymerisation, and the amide links are the same peptide bonds that join amino acids in proteins such as silk.',
 'Hydrogen bonds between the N–H and C=O groups of neighbouring chains make nylon strong and tough. Drawing the fibre out lines the chains up and makes it stronger still.',
 'During the Second World War nylon was needed for parachutes and ropes, so stockings became scarce again. The "6,6" refers to the six carbons in each monomer.'],
 x:'Condensation polymers: polyamides.',k:'polyamides, condensation, hydrogen bonding'},
{s:'kevlar',c:'Materials',t:'A chemist’s cloudy liquid became body armour',
 h:'Kevlar is five times stronger than steel for the same weight.',
 p:['In 1965 Stephanie Kwolek at DuPont was trying to make a strong, light fibre for tyres. One polymer solution came out thin and cloudy, the kind of result usually thrown away. She persuaded a colleague to spin it anyway.',
 'The fibre was astonishingly strong. Kevlar is a polyamide like nylon, but its chains are built from rigid benzene rings joined in a straight line, so they pack together in neat, parallel rows.',
 'Each chain is linked to its neighbours by many hydrogen bonds between the amide groups, and the flat rings stack on top of one another. The result is a fibre that is strong for its weight, heat-resistant and hard to cut.',
 'Kevlar is used in bulletproof vests, helmets, ropes and phone cases. Kwolek received many honours and was inducted into the US National Inventors Hall of Fame.'],
 x:'Polyamides and intermolecular forces in polymers.',k:'polymers, hydrogen bonding, aromatics'},
{s:'silicon-purity',c:'Materials',t:'The purest material humans make',
 h:'Computer chips start as sand and end up 99.9999999% pure.',
 p:['Silicon dioxide, the main compound in sand, is reduced with carbon in an electric furnace to give metallurgical-grade silicon, about 98% pure. That is far from good enough for electronics.',
 'The silicon is turned into trichlorosilane, SiHCl<sub>3</sub>, a liquid that can be purified by distillation, then decomposed back to very pure silicon. Finally a single crystal is pulled slowly from the melt, and the impurities prefer to stay in the liquid.',
 'Silicon for chips is at least "nine nines" pure: no more than about one foreign atom per billion. Then manufacturers deliberately add precise traces of boron or phosphorus to control how it conducts.',
 'Silicon is a semiconductor because its electrons need only a small energy boost to jump into a band where they can move.'],
 x:'Group 14 elements; giant covalent structures.',k:'silicon, purification, semiconductors'},
{s:'dissolving-nobel-medals',c:'History',t:'The chemist who dissolved two Nobel medals',
 h:'In 1940 he hid gold from the Nazis in a beaker on a shelf.',
 p:['When German troops occupied Copenhagen, the chemist George de Hevesy was working at Niels Bohr’s institute. Two German physicists, Max von Laue and James Franck, had sent their gold Nobel medals there for safekeeping, which was illegal under Nazi law.',
 'Gold is very unreactive, but it dissolves in <b>aqua regia</b>, a mixture of concentrated nitric and hydrochloric acids. The nitric acid oxidises a little gold, and the chloride ions trap the Au<sup>3+</sup> as the stable complex ion [AuCl<sub>4</sub>]<sup>−</sup>, pulling the equilibrium forward.',
 'De Hevesy dissolved the medals and left the orange solution on a shelf. The soldiers searched the building and never noticed it.',
 'After the war the gold was recovered from the solution, and the Nobel Foundation recast the medals and presented them again.'],
 x:'Redox, complex ions and shifting equilibria.',k:'redox, complex ions, metals'},
{s:'blue-blood',c:'Biology',t:'Octopuses have blue blood',
 h:'The colour of blood is the colour of a metal complex.',
 p:['Your blood is red because of <b>haemoglobin</b>, a protein carrying iron in a flat ring called haem. Oxygen binds to the iron, and the complex absorbs blue-green light strongly, so blood looks red.',
 'Octopuses, squid, crabs and spiders use <b>haemocyanin</b> instead, which carries oxygen between two copper atoms. Without oxygen it is colourless; with oxygen bound, it is blue.',
 'Both work because transition metal ions can bind small molecules such as O<sub>2</sub> as ligands and release them again, and both are coloured because of electron transitions involving the metal.',
 'Haemocyanin works better than haemoglobin in cold water with little oxygen, which suits many sea creatures.'],
 x:'Transition metals, complexes and ligands.',k:'complex ions, transition metals, colour'},
{s:'oxygen-from-water',c:'Biology',t:'The oxygen you breathe came from water, not carbon dioxide',
 h:'An isotope experiment settled where the oxygen in photosynthesis comes from.',
 p:['The overall equation for photosynthesis is 6CO<sub>2</sub> + 6H<sub>2</sub>O → C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>12</sub>O<sub>6</sub> + 6O<sub>2</sub>. Looking at it, you might guess the oxygen gas comes from the carbon dioxide.',
 'In 1941 Samuel Ruben and Martin Kamen tested this with oxygen-18, a heavy isotope. When they gave algae water labelled with oxygen-18, the oxygen gas released contained it; labelled carbon dioxide did not give labelled oxygen gas.',
 'So the oxygen comes from <b>splitting water</b>, driven by light energy. The oxygen atoms in carbon dioxide end up in the sugar and in new water molecules.',
 'Isotope labelling works because isotopes behave the same chemically but can be told apart by mass, exactly what a mass spectrometer measures.'],
 x:'Isotopes; mass spectrometry; redox.',k:'isotopes, photosynthesis, redox'},
{s:'glass-is-not-liquid',c:'Materials',t:'Old windows are not thicker at the bottom because glass flows',
 h:'A popular myth, and the real explanation.',
 p:['People often say glass is a very slow liquid, and point to old church windows that are thicker at the bottom.',
 'Glass is an <b>amorphous solid</b>: its atoms are arranged randomly like a liquid’s, but locked in place. Calculations suggest that at room temperature, cathedral glass would take far longer than the age of the universe to flow noticeably.',
 'The real reason is how the glass was made. Old window glass was spun or blown and was uneven in thickness, and glaziers usually set the thicker edge at the bottom for stability. Some old panes are thicker at the top or sides.',
 'Glass is mostly silicon dioxide with sodium and calcium oxides added to lower its melting point, which breaks up the regular network found in quartz.'],
 x:'Structure of SiO₂; crystalline and amorphous solids.',k:'solids, silica, structure'},
{s:'salt-on-roads',c:'Everyday',t:'Why salt melts ice on roads',
 h:'Salt does not heat the ice. It lowers the temperature at which water freezes.',
 p:['Ice and liquid water at 0 °C are in dynamic equilibrium: molecules leave the ice to join the liquid at the same rate as they join the ice from the liquid.',
 'Dissolved salt ions get in the way of water molecules joining the ice, but they do not stop molecules leaving it. So ice melts faster than it forms, and the mixture needs to be colder before freezing wins. This is <b>freezing point depression</b>.',
 'It depends on the number of dissolved particles, not their type. NaCl gives two ions per formula unit; calcium chloride gives three, which is why CaCl<sub>2</sub> works at lower temperatures. Below about −10 °C rock salt becomes much less effective.',
 'The same idea explains why the sea freezes at about −2 °C and why antifreeze in car engines works.'],
 x:'Colligative properties; dynamic equilibrium.',k:'solutions, colligative properties, equilibrium'},
{s:'dna-hydrogen-bonds',c:'Biology',t:'Hydrogen bonds hold your DNA together',
 h:'They are weak enough to unzip and strong enough to last.',
 p:['DNA is two strands wound into a double helix. Each strand has a backbone of sugars and phosphates; the bases point inwards and pair up: adenine with thymine, guanine with cytosine.',
 'The pairs are held by <b>hydrogen bonds</b>: two between A and T, three between G and C. Each hydrogen bond is weak compared with a covalent bond, so enzymes can unzip the strands to copy or read them, but millions together hold the helix firmly.',
 'DNA richer in G–C pairs needs a higher temperature to separate, a fact used in PCR, where DNA is heated to about 95 °C to split the strands.',
 'The double helix was published in 1953 by Watson and Crick, using X-ray data from Rosalind Franklin and Maurice Wilkins, including Franklin’s famous Photo 51.'],
 x:'Hydrogen bonding; nucleic acids.',k:'hydrogen bonding, DNA, biomolecules'},
{s:'carbon-monoxide',c:'Biology',t:'Why carbon monoxide is so dangerous',
 h:'It binds to haemoglobin over 200 times more strongly than oxygen does.',
 p:['Haemoglobin picks up oxygen in the lungs and releases it in the tissues. The binding is an equilibrium, so oxygen loads where it is plentiful and unloads where it is scarce.',
 '<b>Carbon monoxide</b> is a ligand too, and it binds to the iron in haem roughly 200–250 times more strongly than oxygen. Even small amounts in the air take over binding sites and stop blood carrying oxygen. It is colourless and odourless, so you cannot sense it.',
 'Treatment uses Le Chatelier’s principle: giving pure oxygen, sometimes at high pressure, pushes the equilibrium back towards oxygen-bound haemoglobin and helps displace the CO.',
 'This is why gas heaters and stoves need ventilation and why carbon monoxide alarms save lives.'],
 x:'Complex ions and ligand exchange; Le Chatelier’s principle.',k:'ligands, equilibrium, haemoglobin'},
{s:'oscillating-reaction',c:'Kinetics',t:'The reaction that ticks like a clock',
 h:'A solution that changes colour back and forth by itself, again and again.',
 p:['In the 1950s Boris Belousov found a mixture of bromate, citric acid and a cerium catalyst whose colour flipped between yellow and colourless every minute or so. Journals rejected his paper, because a reaction going back and forth seemed to break the rules.',
 'Anatol Zhabotinsky later developed it, and it is now called the <b>Belousov–Zhabotinsky reaction</b>. With an iron indicator, it pulses between red and blue, and in a thin dish it makes spreading spiral waves.',
 'It does not break thermodynamics. Overall the reaction always runs downhill in Gibbs energy. But it goes through intermediates whose concentrations rise and fall in a feedback loop: one step makes a product that speeds up its own formation, another removes it.',
 'Oscillating reactions helped scientists understand natural rhythms such as heartbeats and some patterns on animal skins.'],
 x:'Reaction mechanisms, intermediates and catalysis.',k:'kinetics, mechanisms, catalysts'},
{s:'oobleck',c:'Everyday',t:'Cornflour and water: liquid until you punch it',
 h:'You can run across a pool of it, but stand still and you sink.',
 p:['Mix about two parts cornflour with one part water and you get oobleck. Stir it slowly and it flows like a thick liquid; hit it hard and it feels solid.',
 'Cornflour is made of tiny starch grains suspended in the water. When you move slowly, water lubricates the grains and they slide past each other. A sudden force pushes the grains together faster than the water can flow between them, and they jam into a temporary solid.',
 'A liquid whose resistance to flow changes with the force applied is <b>non-Newtonian</b>. Ketchup is the opposite type: it flows more easily when you shake it.',
 'The name comes from a Dr. Seuss book. Researchers study shear-thickening fluids for flexible body armour and protective sports gear.'],
 x:'Intermolecular forces and properties of mixtures.',k:'mixtures, starch, physical properties'},
{s:'catalytic-converter',c:'Environment',t:'Your car’s exhaust passes over platinum',
 h:'A honeycomb with a few grams of precious metal cleans up the worst gases.',
 p:['Car engines produce three main pollutants: carbon monoxide, unburnt hydrocarbons and nitrogen oxides (NOx). A <b>catalytic converter</b> deals with all three.',
 'Exhaust gases flow through a ceramic honeycomb coated with a thin layer of platinum, palladium and rhodium, which gives a huge surface area for a small amount of metal. Gas molecules adsorb onto the metal surface, their bonds weaken, they react, and the products desorb.',
 'Typical reactions are 2CO + 2NO → 2CO<sub>2</sub> + N<sub>2</sub>, and the oxidation of hydrocarbons to CO<sub>2</sub> and water.',
 'Leaded petrol poisons the catalyst by coating the metal surface, one of the reasons lead was removed from petrol. The precious metals also make converters a target for thieves.'],
 x:'Heterogeneous catalysis; nitrogen oxides.',k:'catalysis, pollution, transition metals'},
{s:'chlorine-in-water',c:'Everyday',t:'How chlorine keeps tap water safe',
 h:'The active ingredient is not really chlorine itself.',
 p:['When chlorine is added to water, it reacts in a disproportionation: Cl<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O ⇌ HCl + HOCl. One chlorine atom is reduced to chloride (−1) and the other is oxidised to +1 in chloric(I) acid, HOCl.',
 '<b>HOCl</b> is the main disinfectant. It is a small, uncharged molecule that passes easily through bacterial cell walls and oxidises vital proteins inside.',
 'HOCl is a weak acid, so at higher pH it turns into the chlorate(I) ion, ClO<sup>−</sup>, which kills microbes much less effectively. Pools are kept at about pH 7.2–7.8 to balance disinfection with comfort.',
 'The "swimming pool smell" is not chlorine but chloramines, formed when chlorine reacts with sweat and urine. A strong smell usually means a pool needs more treatment, not less.'],
 x:'Disproportionation; Group 17 and water treatment.',k:'chlorine, redox, weak acids'},
{s:'silver-kills-bacteria',c:'Medicine',t:'Why silver kills bacteria',
 h:'People have used silver to keep water fresh for thousands of years.',
 p:['Silver metal itself is fairly unreactive, but a little dissolves as <b>Ag<sup>+</sup></b> ions, and those ions are toxic to many microbes.',
 'Ag<sup>+</sup> binds strongly to sulfur, especially the thiol groups in the amino acid cysteine. That damages enzymes and proteins in bacterial cell walls and disrupts their metabolism. Human cells tolerate low levels far better.',
 'Silver is used in wound dressings, catheters and some water filters. It is also why silver tarnishes: it reacts with traces of hydrogen sulfide in the air to form black silver sulfide.',
 'Too much silver over a long time can turn skin permanently blue-grey, a condition called argyria, so silver supplements are not a good idea.'],
 x:'Transition metal ions; precipitation of sulfides.',k:'metal ions, sulfur, medicine'},
{s:'hard-water',c:'Everyday',t:'Why kettles get furred up',
 h:'The white crust is rock that was dissolved in your water.',
 p:['Rain is slightly acidic because it dissolves carbon dioxide. As it seeps through limestone (calcium carbonate), it slowly dissolves it: CaCO<sub>3</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O + CO<sub>2</sub> ⇌ Ca(HCO<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>.',
 'Water containing dissolved calcium and magnesium ions is called <b>hard water</b>. Heating it drives the reaction backwards: carbon dioxide is released and calcium carbonate precipitates as limescale on kettles and heating elements.',
 'The same equilibrium, running in each direction over thousands of years, builds stalactites and carves out limestone caves.',
 'Hard water also wastes soap, because calcium ions form an insoluble scum with soap anions. Descalers are weak acids such as citric or ethanoic acid, which react with the carbonate.'],
 x:'Equilibria involving carbonates; Group 2 chemistry.',k:'carbonates, equilibrium, Group 2'},
{s:'mendeleev-gaps',c:'History',t:'Mendeleev predicted elements nobody had seen',
 h:'He left gaps in his table and described what would fill them.',
 p:['In 1869 Dmitri Mendeleev arranged the 63 known elements by atomic mass in rows, so that elements with similar properties lined up in columns. Where the pattern broke, he left gaps rather than force an element into the wrong place.',
 'He predicted the properties of the missing elements. For "eka-silicon", below silicon, he predicted an atomic mass of about 72, a density of about 5.5 g cm<sup>−3</sup> and an oxide with formula EO<sub>2</sub>.',
 'Germanium, discovered in 1886, matched remarkably well: atomic mass 72.6, density 5.3 g cm<sup>−3</sup>, oxide GeO<sub>2</sub>. Gallium and scandium also filled his gaps.',
 'We now order elements by proton number rather than mass, which fixes the few places where Mendeleev had to swap elements, such as tellurium and iodine.'],
 x:'Periodicity and predicting properties from group trends.',k:'periodic table, periodicity, history'},
{s:'invisible-ink',c:'Everyday',t:'Lemon juice invisible ink is just slow burning',
 h:'A secret message that appears when you hold it near heat.',
 p:['Write on paper with lemon juice and let it dry, and the message is invisible. Warm the paper gently near a lamp or iron and the writing turns brown.',
 'Lemon juice contains sugars and citric acid. The acid breaks down the cellulose of the paper where you wrote, and heat makes the sugars and damaged cellulose decompose and char, turning brown well before the rest of the paper does.',
 'In other words, the message is written in "pre-burned" paper: those areas start oxidising and caramelising at a lower temperature.',
 'Milk and onion juice work too. Spies in both World Wars used more sophisticated inks that needed a chemical developer, such as a solution that reacts to form a coloured complex.'],
 x:'Oxidation and decomposition; effect of acid on reactions.',k:'oxidation, sugars, decomposition'},
{s:'chewing-gum-and-oil',c:'Everyday',t:'Why peanut butter gets gum out of hair',
 h:'Grease dissolves grease.',
 p:['Chewing gum base is a mix of synthetic polymers, such as polyisobutylene and polyvinyl acetate, with resins and waxes. They are mostly non-polar and stick to the oils on hair.',
 'Water cannot help, because it is polar and does not mix with the gum. An oily substance such as peanut butter, cooking oil or mayonnaise is non-polar and gets between the gum and the hair, loosening it.',
 'This is "like dissolves like": substances with similar intermolecular forces mix well. Non-polar molecules interact with each other through London forces, and water molecules would rather hydrogen-bond with each other.',
 'Ice works for a different reason: cooling the gum below its glass transition temperature makes it brittle, so it cracks off.'],
 x:'Intermolecular forces and solubility.',k:'polarity, polymers, solubility'},
{s:'dry-ice',c:'Everyday',t:'Dry ice skips the liquid stage',
 h:'Solid carbon dioxide turns straight into gas at −78.5 °C.',
 p:['At normal atmospheric pressure, carbon dioxide cannot exist as a liquid. Warm the solid and it <b>sublimes</b> directly to gas at −78.5 °C, which is why it is called dry ice.',
 'You can see why on a phase diagram: carbon dioxide’s triple point, where solid, liquid and gas coexist, is at about 5.1 atmospheres. Below that pressure, the liquid phase simply does not exist.',
 'The white fog around dry ice is not carbon dioxide gas, which is invisible. It is water vapour from the air condensing into tiny droplets because the cold gas chills it.',
 'Dry ice is used to ship frozen food and vaccines. Carbon dioxide is denser than air, so it collects at floor level, and in a closed space it can displace the oxygen, so it must be used with ventilation.'],
 x:'States of matter and phase diagrams.',k:'phase diagrams, sublimation, gases'},
{s:'activation-energy-matches',c:'Kinetics',t:'Why a match needs a strike',
 h:'The reaction is downhill, but it needs a push over the hill first.',
 p:['Burning wood or a match head is strongly exothermic, so why does a match not burst into flame in the box? Because every reaction has an <b>activation energy</b>: a minimum energy that colliding particles need for bonds to start breaking.',
 'Striking a safety match creates friction heat. A little red phosphorus on the strip is converted to the far more reactive white phosphorus, which ignites in air. That ignites potassium chlorate and sulfur in the match head, which in turn light the wood.',
 'Once burning, the reaction releases enough heat to give neighbouring molecules the activation energy they need, so it keeps itself going.',
 'Safety matches keep the phosphorus on the box and the oxidising agent on the head, so they light only when struck against each other.'],
 x:'Activation energy and the Boltzmann distribution.',k:'activation energy, combustion, kinetics'},
{s:'stomach-acid',c:'Acids',t:'Your stomach is more acidic than lemon juice',
 h:'Stomach acid sits around pH 1.5 to 3.5. Here is why it does not dissolve you.',
 p:['Your stomach makes hydrochloric acid, keeping its contents at roughly pH 1.5–3.5. That kills many microbes and activates the enzyme pepsin, which breaks down proteins.',
 'Lemon juice is about pH 2–2.5, and pH is a log scale, so each unit is a factor of ten in hydrogen ion concentration. Stomach contents at pH 1.5 have about ten times more H<sup>+</sup> than juice at pH 2.5.',
 'The stomach protects itself with a thick layer of mucus and a layer of hydrogencarbonate ions right next to its wall, which neutralise acid before it reaches the cells. The cells lining it are also replaced every few days.',
 'Antacids are bases such as magnesium hydroxide or calcium carbonate that neutralise excess acid. Heartburn happens when acid escapes upwards into the oesophagus, which lacks that protection.'],
 x:'pH, strong acids and neutralisation.',k:'pH, acids, neutralisation'}
];
